{
  "program": "CBSTM03B",
  "caseId": "case20",
  "description": "Four independent two-byte FILE STATUS areas, proven as a ten-call interleaving - app/cbl/CBSTM03B.CBL:83-97, gate G47. THE AREAS. The program declares one 01 group per SELECT and never shares a status between paragraphs: TRNXFILE-STATUS (:83-85), XREFFILE-STATUS (:87-89), CUSTFILE-STATUS (:91-93) and ACCTFILE-STATUS (:95-97), each two PIC X bytes and none of them carrying a VALUE clause (practice B7). Each of the four paragraphs ends by moving its OWN area into the caller's LK-M03B-RC and no other - 1900-EXIT MOVE TRNXFILE-STATUS (:151-152), 2900-EXIT MOVE XREFFILE-STATUS (:175-176), 3900-EXIT MOVE CUSTFILE-STATUS (:200-201) and 4900-EXIT MOVE ACCTFILE-STATUS (:225-226). Independence is therefore a claim about four separate spans, and a claim of that shape cannot be established one file at a time: it needs a single run in which the areas hold DIFFERENT values simultaneously, which is what these ten calls produce and why the transcript is one ordered sequence rather than four short ones. THE SCRIPT, CALL BY CALL. Calls 0 to 3 open all four DDs in dispatch order - EVALUATE LK-M03B-DD (:118) matching WHEN 'TRNXFILE' (:119), WHEN 'XREFFILE' (:121), WHEN 'CUSTFILE' (:123) and WHEN 'ACCTFILE' (:125) - and each OPEN INPUT (:136, :160, :184, :209) answers '00' on its own area. Call 4 closes CUSTFILE alone: IF M03B-CLOSE (:195) is reached only after IF M03B-OPEN (:183) and IF M03B-READ-K (:188) have both tested false, CLOSE CUST-FILE (:196) answers '00', and the other three files stay open and untouched. Call 5 then reads the closed CUSTFILE by key, and this time IF M03B-READ-K (:188) IS matched, so MOVE LK-M03B-KEY (1:LK-M03B-KEY-LN) TO FD-CUST-ID (:189) and READ CUST-FILE INTO LK-M03B-FLDT (:190) both execute - and a read of a file that is not open answers '47' and delivers no record, after which GO TO 3900-EXIT (:192) carries that '47' into LK-M03B-RC (:201). Calls 6, 7 and 8 read the three files that are still open - sequential READ TRNX-FILE INTO LK-M03B-FLDT (:141), sequential READ XREF-FILE INTO LK-M03B-FLDT (:165), and keyed MOVE (:214) then READ ACCT-FILE INTO LK-M03B-FLDT (:215) - and every one of them answers '00' and returns its record. Call 9 repeats call 5 and answers '47' again. THE CRUX. At calls 6, 7 and 8 the CUSTFILE area holds '47' while the TRNXFILE, XREFFILE and ACCTFILE areas simultaneously hold '00', and all three reads succeed with a record each. That is the assertion: a failure on one file neither perturbs another file's status nor suppresses another file's read. A translation that kept one shared status field - the obvious simplification, since four two-byte spans look like duplication - would answer '47' to call 6, or would answer '00' to call 9 because a later success had overwritten the earlier failure, and either way it would pass every one of the nineteen single-file cases in this folder and fail only here. Call 9 closes the remaining hole: it proves the '47' is still there AFTER three successful reads on three other files, so the case asserts persistence and not merely coexistence. WHY '47' IS THE RIGHT STATUS AND WHY IT IS NOT AN EXCEPTION. A READ against a file not open in INPUT or I-O mode is status 47, and StatementGenerationJobB.NOT_OPEN_FOR_READ_STATUS carries exactly that two-byte value. It reaches the caller the way every other status does, through the paragraph's own nnn900-EXIT MOVE, because CBSTM03B has no error path of any kind: it contains no DISPLAY, no CALL, no abend and no RETURN-CODE reference, and deciding what a status MEANS is the caller's job. app/cbl/CBSTM03A.CBL:379-386 admits only WHEN '00' from a CUSTFILE keyed read and sends WHEN OTHER to DISPLAY 'ERROR READING CUSTFILE' (:383) and PERFORM 9999-ABEND-PROGRAM (:385), so a Java implementation that threw here instead of answering '47' would bypass the caller's own guard and abend in the wrong place, with the wrong message, at the wrong severity. ORDER IS BEHAVIOUR (gate G30, rule R7). The EVALUATE arms are tested in source order and WHEN OTHER (:127) is last, so a DD is recognised by its name and by nothing else; and inside each paragraph the three IF ... END-IF blocks carry no ELSE, so the CLOSE guard is reachable only after the OPEN and read guards have been evaluated and failed. Call 4 exercises that reachability on CUSTFILE and call 5 depends on call 4 having happened - the '47' is a consequence of the preceding CLOSE, not a property of the DD - which is why the ten calls form one ordered transcript and why reordering them would change what they assert. WHAT THE CALLER ESTABLISHES BEFORE EACH CALL. All ten of these calls apply both of app/cbl/CBSTM03A.CBL's resets: MOVE ZERO TO WS-M03B-RC (:349, :375, :399, :733, :768, :786, :804, :859, :876, :892) and MOVE SPACES TO WS-M03B-FLDT (:350, :376, :400, :745, :834). Each call therefore arrives with LK-M03B-RC = '00' and a blank 1000-byte record area, and every value in the transcript is one CBSTM03B WROTE on that call rather than one carried in. That is the deliberate complement of the two other shapes in this folder. case19 leaves the status un-zeroed so that WHEN OTHER's failure to write it is observable at all: an unrecognised DD reaches GO TO 9999-GOBACK (:127-128), jumps past all four nnn900-EXIT paragraphs, and leaves LK-M03B-RC exactly as the caller left it. case06 and case14 prove the middle shape, where a recognised DD carrying an operation its paragraph has no guard for performs no I/O and yet still has its nnn900-EXIT overwrite the status from the file's unchanged area. This case is a third thing again: every DD here is recognised AND every operation is one that DD's paragraph guards, so real I/O happens on all ten calls and all ten statuses are freshly written, from four different areas. WHAT IS SUPPLIED AND WHAT IS CONSULTED. Calls 5 and 9 carry '000000001' left-justified in the 25-byte LK-M03B-KEY span with LK-M03B-KEY-LN = +9, which is what app/cbl/CBSTM03A.CBL:372-374 establishes as MOVE XREF-CUST-ID TO WS-M03B-KEY then COMPUTE WS-M03B-KEY-LN = LENGTH OF XREF-CUST-ID; call 8 carries '00000000001' with +11, which is :397-398's COMPUTE WS-M03B-KEY-LN = LENGTH OF XREF-ACCT-ID. Both keys name row 0 of their seeded dataset - CUST-ID 000000001 and ACCT-ID 00000000001 - so the CUSTFILE '47' is unambiguously about the closed file and not about an absent record, and the ACCTFILE '00' is a real hit on a real key. LK-M03B-KEY-LN is PIC S9(4) with no USAGE clause (:111), so it is four zoned DISPLAY bytes with the sign overpunched into the low-order byte rather than a two-byte halfword: +0 is '000{', +9 is '000I' and +11 is '001A'. The seven non-keyed calls carry 25 spaces and '000{'. WHAT IS PINNED. All ten LK-M03B-AREA images are pinned twice over: span by span under the six names of the LINKAGE SECTION, because FieldDiffer compares field by field and never as a whole string (gate G17, practice B8), and as the complete 1040-byte image, which is what proves the TOTAL width and therefore that every span was emitted at its declared length (gates G19 and G21). 1040 = 8 + 1 + 2 + 25 + 4 + 1000 is re-derivable by hand from :100-112 with no tooling (practice B11), and the three record areas the successful reads deliver are the meaningful prefixes those widths predict - 350 bytes of a TRNXFILE row, 50 of an XREFFILE row and 300 of an ACCTFILE row, each followed by spaces out to 1000. The subroutine returns raw space-padded spans and decodes nothing into a model type, which is why an expectation states the span and not an object. THE READ-ONLY PROOF. All four datasets are read back afterwards and pinned field by field across every span of their copybooks - TRNXFILE's fourteen from app/cpy/COSTM01.CPY, XREFFILE's four from app/cpy/CVACT03Y.cpy, CUSTFILE's nineteen from app/cpy/CUSTREC.cpy and ACCTFILE's thirteen from app/cpy/CVACT01Y.cpy, the trailing FILLER included in each, and each dataset's row count and record width declared as well. All four SELECTs are OPEN INPUT (:136, :160, :184, :209) and no paragraph contains a WRITE or a REWRITE, so a dataset that came out different from the way it went in would be a parity failure no area image could reveal. Two field names are load-bearing and neither may be tidied (implicit requirement I1): CUSTREC's date of birth is CUST-DOB-YYYYMMDD, one unbroken token, and never CVCUS01Y's CUST-DOB-YYYY-MM-DD; and CVACT01Y's expiry span is ACCT-EXPIRAION-DATE, misspelled in the copybook and therefore misspelled here, because correcting it would leave the diff hunting for a field the decoder never produces. SEED. TRNXFILE's two rows are supplied inline at a full 350 bytes each, both on card 0500024453765740, because no shipped fixture carries the reporting-altered COSTM01 layout. XREFFILE, CUSTFILE and ACCTFILE take rows 0 and 1 of src/test/resources/fixtures/cardxref.txt, custdata.txt and acctdata.txt, copied unchanged from app/data/ASCII. cardxref.txt ships 36 bytes per row and omits CVACT03Y's trailing FILLER PIC X(14), so the CARDXREF_FILLER_PAD_36_TO_50 normalisation is declared and the harness supplies that FILLER once, at seed time, before anything reads the row (gate G16); it is one of only two pads in the whole suite, the other being USRSEC 57 to 80. Every byte is US-ASCII, stated explicitly and never taken from the platform default (practice B8). THE UNIT AND WHAT IS ABSENT. unitKind is COMPONENT: StatementGenerationJobB's prompt-mandated ...Job name is honoured verbatim under rule R1 while its nature is a Spring @Component and not a Spring Batch Job (gate G12, implicit requirement I3) - the source header reads 'Type : BATCH COBOL Subroutine', no EXEC PGM=CBSTM03B exists anywhere in app/jcl, app/proc or app/csd, and CBSTM03A calls it thirteen times (:351, :377, :401, :734, :746, :769, :787, :805, :835, :860, :877, :893, :909). That divergence is recorded rather than quietly resolved (practice B4). The unit is reached through its typed method with no JobLauncher, no MockMvc and no servlet container in the path (gate G51), which is also why jobParameters is empty: a job parameter comes from a JCL PARM and this subroutine has no way to receive one, so the DD name, the operation, the key and the key length all travel in the linkage area the transcript pins. expectedReturnCode is 0 because CBSTM03B never assigns RETURN-CODE - the two-byte LK-M03B-RC is a FILE STATUS carried in the linkage area, and the two are never conflated. expectedMessages is empty because the program contains no DISPLAY statement at all. Operation codes 'W' (:107) and 'Z' (:108) are declared in the linkage area and tested by no IF in any of the four paragraphs; they stay declared and unimplemented (practice B5) and this case drives neither, its ten calls using only OPEN, CLOSE, sequential READ and keyed READ-K. WHEN OTHER is not driven here either - that arm belongs to case19, and it is the one path through this program on which LK-M03B-RC is not written at all. PROVENANCE (practice B12, risk R-A). Every expected value above was derived STATICALLY, by structured reading of the line numbers cited, the byte offsets of the four copybooks and the real bytes of the shipped ASCII fixtures, and was NEVER captured from, recorded against or replayed from any execution of the COBOL. That execution is empirically impossible in this environment: cobc refuses CBSTM03B at :116 because PROCEDURE DIVISION USING LK-M03B-AREA is a subprogram entry rather than an executable main, its indexed file handler is reported disabled while all four of these SELECTs are ORGANIZATION INDEXED, and no Language Environment CEE service exists. AAP section 0.7.6 records the eight independently verified blockers and section 0.9.11 escalates the substitution for explicit confirmation rather than absorbing it silently; only the PROVENANCE of these values changes - twenty cases per program, field-for-field diffing and the diff-count-equals-zero gate per module all stand. No user rule governs this fixture: review_rules returns exactly 'No user rules provided.', so AAP section 0.10.2's enterprise practices supply the standard instead, and their absence is not treated as licence to assert less. HOW THE CALL SCRIPT IS DECLARED RATHER THAN HELD IN JAVA: the sequence of CALLs below used to live in a Java switch over this file's case identifier, which meant the case file did not describe the one thing it asserts - a subroutine whose entire contract is a DD name plus a one-character operation code IS its call sequence. Renumbering this file would have silently given it a different script, and a twenty-first case would have thrown from a default arm. unitStimulus.operationScript now carries it, one entry per CALL in issue order, each declaring LK-M03B-DD, the 88-level operation code from app/cbl/CBSTM03B.cbl:103-108, and where applicable LK-M03B-KEY and LK-M03B-KEY-LN. Two caller idioms are declared explicitly because the source distinguishes them: status '00' is the MOVE ZERO TO WS-M03B-RC every real call site issues, and its ABSENCE carries the previous call's status forward - the only way to observe a fall-through exit that assigns nothing new; primesRecordArea true is the MOVE SPACES TO WS-M03B-FLDT that app/cbl/CBSTM03A.CBL:L350, :L745 and :L834 issue, and its ABSENCE is the omission at :L857-860 that makes 'at end of file the record area is returned unchanged' an assertion with content. Every expected area image below was derived from this script by reading CBSTM03B's rules, never by running the Java.",
